{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"shared-weak-ptr","title":"shared_ptr & weak_ptr","group":"Copy, Move & Smart Pointers","order":36,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>std::shared_ptr</code> allows <em>shared</em> ownership through reference counting; the object is destroyed when the last owner lets go. <code>std::weak_ptr</code> observes without owning — the cure for reference cycles.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-users\"></i>Reference counting</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nstruct Texture {\n    std::string file;\n    explicit Texture(std::string f) : file(std::move(f)) {}\n    ~Texture() { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"unload \" &lt;&lt; file &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    auto tex = std::make_shared&lt;Texture&gt;(\"grass.png\");   // count = 1\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; tex.use_count() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    {\n        auto sprite1 = tex;                               // count = 2\n        std::shared_ptr&lt;Texture&gt; sprite2 = tex;           // count = 3\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; tex.use_count() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    }                                                     // back to 1\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; tex.use_count() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    tex.reset();                                          // 0 → destroyed\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"done\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1\n3\n1\nunload grass.png\ndone</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-settings\"></i>How it works</h3>\n<p>A <code>shared_ptr</code> is two pointers: one to the object, one to a <strong>control block</strong> holding the strong count, the weak count and the deleter. <code>make_shared</code> allocates object and control block in <em>one</em> allocation.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>unique_ptr</code></th><th><code>shared_ptr</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Owners</td><td>Exactly one</td><td>Many</td></tr><tr><td>Copyable</td><td>No (move only)</td><td>Yes (increments the count)</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>1 pointer</td><td>2 pointers</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>Free</td><td>Atomic increments/decrements on copy/destroy</td></tr><tr><td>Default choice?</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>Only when ownership is genuinely shared</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-refresh-alert\"></i>Cycles leak — <code>weak_ptr</code> breaks them</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nstruct Employee;\nstruct Team {\n    std::string name;\n    std::shared_ptr&lt;Employee&gt; lead;         // owns its lead\n    ~Team() { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"~Team\\n\"; }\n};\nstruct Employee {\n    std::string name;\n    std::weak_ptr&lt;Team&gt; team;               // observes: does NOT keep the team alive\n    ~Employee() { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"~Employee\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    auto team = std::make_shared&lt;Team&gt;();\n    auto ada = std::make_shared&lt;Employee&gt;();\n    team-&gt;name = \"core\";\n    team-&gt;lead = ada;\n    ada-&gt;team = team;\n    if (auto t = ada-&gt;team.lock())          // lock(): shared_ptr if still alive, else empty\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; \"ada is in \" &lt;&lt; t-&gt;name &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; team.use_count() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; ada.use_count() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}   // both destroyed; with shared_ptr in both directions neither destructor would run</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>ada is in core\n1 2\n~Team\n~Employee</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::weak_ptr&lt;int&gt; watcher;\n    {\n        auto p = std::make_shared&lt;int&gt;(42);\n        watcher = p;\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; watcher.expired() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; *watcher.lock() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    }\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; watcher.expired() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (watcher.lock() == nullptr) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0 42\n1 1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-back\"></i><code>enable_shared_from_this</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\n\nstruct Session : std::enable_shared_from_this&lt;Session&gt; {\n    std::shared_ptr&lt;Session&gt; self() { return shared_from_this(); }   // NOT shared_ptr&lt;Session&gt;(this)!\n};\n\nint main() {\n    auto s = std::make_shared&lt;Session&gt;();\n    auto again = s-&gt;self();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; s.use_count() &lt;&lt; '\\n';     // shares the same control block\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Never create two <code>shared_ptr</code>s from the same raw pointer (<code>std::shared_ptr&lt;T&gt; a(raw), b(raw);</code>) — each gets its own control block and both delete the object. Create once with <code>make_shared</code> and copy the <code>shared_ptr</code>.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Is shared_ptr thread-safe?</summary><div><p>The reference count is updated atomically, so copying and destroying <em>different</em> <code>shared_ptr</code> instances that share an object is safe across threads. Accessing the <em>pointed-to object</em>, or modifying the <em>same</em> <code>shared_ptr</code> instance from several threads, still needs synchronisation (C++20 offers <code>std::atomic&lt;std::shared_ptr&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code>).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When would you use weak_ptr?</summary><div><p>To break ownership cycles (child → parent back-links, observer lists), and for caches that should not keep objects alive: <code>lock()</code> tells you whether the object still exists and gives you temporary ownership while you use it.</p></div></details></section>"}